  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (through Monday)...\n\nA quiet weather pattern for the area through Thursday, with\nridging aloft transitioning to southwesterly flow Thursday\nafternoon. Afternoon temperatures will be in the 60s on Wednesday,\nclimbing into the mid 70s for Thursday afternoon with plenty of\nsunshine each day. \n\nChanges arrive Thursday night of western mountains and foothills\nlocations as a storm system moves into the region. This system is\ncomposed of two pieces, a closed low moving northeast out of\nCalifornia, and a colder Pacific trof dropping southeast out of\nthe Gulf of Alaska. Overnight Thursday into Friday morning these\nsystems merge over western Montana and slide east into our area\nbefore sunrise Friday morning. Models are advertising a strong\nshot of northerly winds driving much colder into the area through\nthe day Friday. These upslope winds combined with atmospheric\nmoisture levels (PWATs) near an inch and strong QG forcing aloft\nwill bring strong chances for precipitation to the area Friday\nthrough Friday night, tapering off from west to east Saturday\nmorning. Probabilities for more than half an inch of precipitation\nduring this period are over 60% from Big Horn county west into \nthe mountains and foothills around Livingston, with the high end\nof the reasonable range sitting at well over an inch in these\nareas. Much lesser amounts of precipitation are expected the \nfurther east you go from Rosebud county into the Dakotas.\n\nThis isn't a terribly cold system compared to what can occur this\ntime of year, but snow levels do drop from around 10kft Friday\nmorning, to between 5-6kft by midnight Friday night, and further\ndown to between 4-5kft as precipitation tapers off Saturday\nmorning. At this point expect impactful snowfall to stay above\n5000 feet with this system, with the higher totals up to 8 inches\nfalling above 7000 feet in the Beartooth/Absaroka/Crazy mountains.\nThe latest forecast is showing only 2 to 4 inches for the Bighorn\nmountains above 7000 feet. \n\nMentioned the strong push of northerly winds, and the\nprobabilities for gusts over 40 mph are substantial in many places\nacross the forecast area Friday afternoon into Friday night.\nCombined with the high precipitation potential and cold air\nintrusion, it will be a blustery afternoon and night across the\nforecast area. Those with Friday night football plans should be\nplanning for the cold windy and wet conditions. \n\nWith this system still several days out there is still time for\nadjustments to the forecast, so stay tuned through the week as\ndetail firm up. \n\nThis system doesn't hang around long and by Saturday afternoon\nprecipitation chances end with sunny conditions developing.\nSeasonal temperatures in the 50s for highs will stick around \nthrough early next week with mainly dry conditions. Lows will\nhover near freezing each morning. Chambers",
